[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: OrangePi Zero3 LTE router: simei user guide
VERSION: 1.0
DATE: 2026-09-03

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
This guide explains how to operate the OrangePi Zero3 router (OpenWrt 25.12.1) with the
Quectel EG25-G modem and its embedded eUICC: how to reach the router, how to manage eSIM
profiles, and how to keep one IMEI per SIM profile with the **simei** tool.

Changing an IMEI is illegal in some countries. simei writes whatever IMEI you assign to a SIM
profile into your own modem; the responsibility for using it is yours.
<<<

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Reaching the router

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The board joins the home LAN as a DHCP client with hostname ``orangepi-lte``. Its current
address is shown by the home router's DHCP table; from the Mac it is kept in ``tools/router.env``.
Over Tailscale the board is always ``orangepi-lte`` (100.111.153.32).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ACCESS-1
TITLE: Web interface (LuCI)
STATEMENT: >>>
Open ``http://<router-ip>/`` in a browser. Log in as ``root`` with an empty password until a
password is set (LuCI > System > Administration). Use plain ``http``: the web TUI is embedded
over http and browsers refuse to embed it inside an https page.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ACCESS-2
TITLE: ssh
STATEMENT: >>>
``ssh root@<router-ip>`` works without a password from the Mac (key ``~/.ssh/id_ed25519``) and
from the phone with the key ``~/.ssh/orangepi_phone_ed25519`` (import the private key into the
phone's ssh client). Over the tailnet: ``ssh root@orangepi-lte`` (Tailscale SSH is enabled, no
key needed from tailnet devices).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ACCESS-3
TITLE: Web terminals
STATEMENT: >>>
``http://<router-ip>:7681/`` opens simei directly in the browser (also reachable as
LuCI > Modem > SIM / IMEI (TUI)). ``http://<router-ip>:7682/`` opens a root shell in the browser.
Both are reachable from the LAN and the tailnet only.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ACCESS-4
TITLE: Raw AT commands from another computer
STATEMENT: >>>
The modem's AT port is exposed on TCP port 5000 by the ``atbridge`` service:
``socat - TCP:<router-ip>:5000`` then type ``AT+GSN`` (Enter). One client at a time.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ACCESS-5
TITLE: Serial console
STATEMENT: >>>
With the USB serial adapter connected to the Mac: ``tools/serial.sh console`` (exit with Ctrl-]),
or ``tools/serial.sh run "ip addr"`` for one command. picocom must be closed first.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: simei: concepts

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
simei keeps a **profile** for every SIM you own. A profile is identified by the SIM's ICCID and
holds a name, the IMEI to use with that SIM, and optionally an APN. Profiles live in
``/etc/config/simei``.

The modem carries an eUICC, so "inserting another SIM" means **enabling another eSIM profile**.
simei talks to the eUICC through ``lpac`` and to the modem through the AT bridge.

Two operations do the real work:

- ``apply`` looks at the SIM the modem currently sees, finds its profile, and if the profile's
  IMEI differs from the modem's IMEI it writes the IMEI (``AT+EGMR=1,7``), restarts the modem
  (``AT+CFUN=1,1``) and verifies the result.
- ``switch`` enables an eSIM profile, restarts the modem and then runs ``apply`` for it.

Reassigning an IMEI between two SIMs is therefore: ``move`` (or ``swap``) the IMEI between the
two profiles, then ``switch`` to (or ``apply`` on) the SIM that should carry it now.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Using the TUI

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: TUI-1
TITLE: Starting the TUI
STATEMENT: >>>
Run ``simei`` over ssh, on the serial console, or open ``http://<router-ip>:7681/``. The main
menu offers: Status, Live dashboard, SIM profiles, Register the inserted SIM, Reassign IMEI,
Apply, eSIM profiles, AT command, Restart, Exit. Menus are numbered; type the number or the
name and press Enter (on a dumb console), or use the arrow keys (whiptail).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: TUI-2
TITLE: Status
STATEMENT: >>>
Shows the modem IMEI (flagged when blank or invalid), SIM ICCID and IMSI, SIM state, signal,
operator and access technology, registration state, and the matched profile with whether its
IMEI matches the modem.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: TUI-3
TITLE: Live dashboard
STATEMENT: >>>
Refreshes every second: download and upload rate of ``wwan0`` and session totals (read from the
kernel counters, no modem round trips), LTE signal (RSSI, RSRP, RSRQ, SNR, from QMI every 3 s),
IMEI, ICCID, operator. Leave with Enter (or any key where the terminal supports raw mode).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: TUI-4
TITLE: eSIM profiles menu
STATEMENT: >>>
Lists every profile on the eUICC with its state, name/nickname and the IMEI assigned to it.
Selecting a profile offers: **Switch** (enable it, restart the modem, write its IMEI),
Enable, Disable, **Assign / change the IMEI for this SIM**, Set nickname, Delete. The menu also
offers **Download a new profile** (activation code ``LPA:1$<smdp>$<matching-id>`` and, if the
operator gave one, a confirmation code) and eUICC info (EID, free memory).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: TUI-5
TITLE: Reassign IMEI
STATEMENT: >>>
Choose Move (source loses the IMEI, target receives it) or Swap, then the two profiles. If the
SIM currently active is affected, simei offers to write the IMEI to the modem immediately.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: TUI-6
TITLE: Apply
STATEMENT: >>>
Shows the planned change (``IMEI change: <old> -> <new>``) and asks for confirmation before
writing. The modem restarts; the screen reports ``verified: modem IMEI is now ...`` when done.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Using the command line

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: CLI-1
TITLE: Command reference
STATEMENT: >>>
.. code-block:: text

    simei status [--raw]           modem + SIM status, matched profile
    simei live [--once]            live bandwidth / signal dashboard
    simei list                     SIM profiles
    simei register [name]          profile for the SIM the modem sees now
    simei add <name> <iccid> [imei] [apn]
    simei set <profile> name|imei|apn|iccid <value>
    simei delete <profile>
    simei move <from> <to>         hand the IMEI to another profile
    simei swap <a> <b>             exchange the IMEIs
    simei apply [--yes] [--dry-run]
    simei switch <iccid|profile> [--yes]
    simei esim [list] | info | enable <iccid> | disable <iccid>
    simei esim delete <iccid> [--yes] | nickname <iccid> <name>
    simei esim download 'LPA:1$smdp$id' [confirmation-code]
    simei at "AT+..."              raw AT command
    simei luhn <14|15 digits>      IMEI check digit
    simei restart modem|wwan|bridge
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: CLI-2
TITLE: First SIM (the DENT eSIM already on the eUICC)
STATEMENT: >>>
.. code-block:: text

    simei register DENT                 # creates sim1 for ICCID 8948010010087197546
    simei set sim1 imei 35xxxxxxxxxxxxx  # 14 digits: the check digit is appended for you
    simei apply                          # writes it, restarts the modem, verifies

The modem shipped with IMEI 000000000000000 (blank); simei refuses to store or back up that
value, so an IMEI has to be assigned once before the first ``apply``.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: CLI-3
TITLE: Adding a second eSIM and moving between them
STATEMENT: >>>
.. code-block:: text

    simei esim download 'LPA:1$smdp.example.com$ABCD-1234'   # from the operator's QR code
    simei esim list                                          # note the new ICCID (disabled)
    simei add "Work" <iccid> 35yyyyyyyyyyyyy                 # its own IMEI
    simei switch <iccid>                                     # enable + restart + write IMEI
    simei switch sim1                                        # back to DENT

To give the second SIM the IMEI of the first: ``simei move sim1 sim2`` then ``simei switch sim2``.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: CLI-4
TITLE: Exit codes and logs
STATEMENT: >>>
``apply`` / ``switch``: 0 done or nothing to do; 1 cancelled or usage error; 2 the IMEI fails the
15-digit Luhn check; 3 no SIM, no profile, or no IMEI assigned; 4 the modem refused the write or did
not come back; 5 the AT bridge or lpac is unreachable. Every write is logged to the system log
(``logread | grep simei``). The first valid IMEI seen is saved to ``/etc/simei.factory-imei``.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Automatic mode

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: AUTO-1
TITLE: auto_apply
STATEMENT: >>>
``uci set simei.global.auto_apply=1; uci commit simei`` makes the hotplug script
``/etc/hotplug.d/tty/90-simei`` run ``simei apply --yes`` about 20 s after the modem's AT port
appears (boot, modem restart). The profile of whatever SIM is active then gets its IMEI written
without asking. Default is off.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Other modem tools on the router

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
- LuCI > Modem > **3ginfo-lite**: signal, band, cell, operator, connection details.
- LuCI > Modem > **SMS tool**: read and send SMS, USSD, send AT commands from the browser.
- LuCI > Network > Interfaces > **wwan**: the LTE connection (QMI, APN ``internet``); change the
  APN there if the operator needs another one.
- ``uqmi -d /dev/cdc-wdm0 --get-signal-info`` and ``--get-serving-system`` from a shell.
- LuCI > Modem > **Preferred LTE/5G Bands**: band locking through ``modemband`` (both packages come
  from the 4IceG apk repository, installed by ``tools/05-modemband.sh``); from a shell
  ``modemband.sh getbands`` and ``modemband.sh setbands "3 7 20"``. The raw equivalent is
  ``simei at 'AT+QCFG="band",0,0x80000,0'`` (see the Quectel EG25-G AT manual). modemband and
  the SMS tool use ``/dev/ttyUSB2``, the port behind the AT bridge: one client at a time.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Maintenance from the Mac

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: MAINT-1
TITLE: Updating simei on the router
STATEMENT: >>>
.. code-block:: text

    sh simei/tests/run.sh          # local tests (socat, jq required)
    tools/rt push simei /          # copy the tree to the router
    tools/rt 'sh /usr/lib/simei/tests/run.sh'   # tests on the router

Everything is versioned in the private repository ``github.com/jftochka/owrt-orangepi-lte``.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: MAINT-2
TITLE: Re-creating the router from scratch
STATEMENT: >>>
Flash the official OpenWrt 25.12.1 image for xunlong_orangepi-zero3, connect serial and Ethernet,
then run in order: ``tools/01-bootstrap-net.sh``, ``tools/rt - < tools/02-install.sh``,
``tools/rt - < tools/05-modemband.sh`` (4IceG apk repository: ``modemband`` + LuCI band page),
``tools/rt push simei /``, ``tools/rt - < tools/03-configure.sh``, ``tools/rt - < tools/04-tailscale.sh``
(open the printed login URL once). If the modem is in ECM mode
(``AT+QCFG="usbnet"`` returns 1) switch it to QMI with ``AT+QCFG="usbnet",0`` and ``AT+CFUN=1,1``.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Troubleshooting

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
- **simei: cannot reach the AT bridge**: ``/etc/init.d/atbridge restart``; check ``ls /dev/ttyUSB*``.
- **Registration: searching / No Service**: ``simei at 'AT+QENG="servingcell"'`` shows whether a
  cell is seen; a blank IMEI can be rejected by the network - assign one and ``simei apply``.
- **lpac: euicc_init**: first check that the modem sees the eUICC at all: ``simei at 'AT+QSIMSTAT?'``
  must answer ``1`` in the second field and ``AT+CPIN?`` must not return CME ERROR 10 or 13;
  otherwise reseat the card and ``simei at 'AT+CFUN=1,1'``. Then: another program holds
  ``/dev/ttyUSB3`` or echo is on; ``simei esim info`` sends ``ATE0`` first. The ``at`` backend is
  the default; the feed's uqmi driver needs ``LPAC_QMI_DEV``, which simei sets but the stock
  ``/usr/bin/lpac`` wrapper does not (SFT-142).
- **Web TUI page empty in LuCI**: LuCI was opened over https; use ``http://`` or the direct link
  shown on the page.
- **Board unreachable after a DHCP change**: look up the new address (home router, or
  ``tools/serial.sh run "ip -4 addr show br-lan"``) and update ``tools/router.env``.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]
